          </p>

          <div class="meta">
            <span>Updated: 17 August 2026</span>
            <span>•</span>
            <span>Habitat IAS</span>
            <span>•</span>
            <span>JPSC Civil Services</span>
          </div>

          <div class="buttons">
            <a class="btn yellow" href="#papers">Find the Papers ↓</a>
            <a class="btn outline" href="/book/">JPSC PYQ Book →</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="notice">
        <div class="container">
          <div class="notice-box">
            <strong>Official source first:</strong>
            JPSC maintains a dedicated
            <a href="https://www.jpsc.gov.in/sam_question_paper.php" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
              Previous Exam Question Paper
            </a>
            section. Use that archive when the required paper is available and
            always verify the examination year and paper before relying on a PDF.
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="stats">
        <div class="container stats-grid">
          <div><b>01</b><span>Find</span><p>Locate the correct JPSC paper from the official archive.</p></div>
          <div><b>02</b><span>Solve</span><p>Attempt the paper under realistic examination conditions.</p></div>
          <div><b>03</b><span>Map</span><p>Connect every question to the syllabus and topic.</p></div>
          <div><b>04</b><span>Improve</span><p>Use errors to change revision and mock-test priorities.</p></div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="content">
        <div class="container layout">
          <aside class="toc">
            <strong>ON THIS PAGE</strong>
            <a href="#why">Why PYQs matter</a>
            <a href="#papers">Where to find papers</a>
            <a href="#latest">Latest-paper strategy</a>
            <a href="#yearwise">Year-wise approach</a>
            <a href="#paper1">Paper 1 analysis</a>
            <a href="#paper2">Paper 2 analysis</a>
            <a href="#mapping">Topic mapping</a>
            <a href="#questiontypes">Question types</a>
            <a href="#solve">How to solve</a>
            <a href="#error">Error log</a>
            <a href="#revision">Revision</a>
            <a href="#mock">PYQ + Mock</a>
            <a href="#book">PYQ Book</a>
            <a href="#mistakes">Mistakes</a>
            <a href="#plan">30-day plan</a>
            <a href="#faq">FAQs</a>
          </aside>

          <article>
            <p class="lead">
              A previous-year paper is not merely a practice sheet. It is one of
              the best available indicators of what the commission has actually
              converted from a broad syllabus into an examination question.
            </p>

            <h2 id="why">1. Why JPSC PYQs Are So Important</h2>

            <p>
              Syllabus tells you the possible universe of preparation.
              <strong>PYQs show the exam's historical selection from that
              universe.</strong> That distinction should change how you prepare.
            </p>

            <div class="why">
              <div><b>DEMAND</b><strong>What does JPSC ask?</strong><span>See the actual wording and subject areas.</span></div>
              <div><b>DEPTH</b><strong>How deeply?</strong><span>Observe whether questions are direct, conceptual or statement-based.</span></div>
              <div><b>REPETITION</b><strong>What returns?</strong><span>Find recurring themes and related concepts.</span></div>
              <div><b>PRIORITY</b><strong>What matters?</strong><span>Use evidence to prioritise revision.</span></div>
            </div>

            <div class="formula">
              <strong>Syllabus → PYQ → Topic Map → Study → Revision → Mock → Error Analysis</strong>
              <span>This is the complete loop.</span>
            </div>

            <h2 id="papers">2. JPSC Previous Year Papers: Where to Find Them</h2>

            <p>
              The safest starting point is the official JPSC question-paper
              archive. It is preferable to treating a third-party PDF as the
              original source. Third-party repositories can still be useful for
              convenient indexing, but verify the paper against the official
              record whenever possible. citeturn0search6turn0search0
            </p>

            <div class="official">
              <div>
                <span>OFFICIAL JPSC</span>
                <strong>Previous Exam Question Paper</strong>
                <p>
                  Open the official archive and select the examination/paper
                  relevant to your preparation.
                </p>
              </div>
              <a
                class="btn blue"
                href="https://www.jpsc.gov.in/sam_question_paper.php"
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener"
              >
                Open Official Archive →
              </a>
            </div>

            <div class="paper-table">
              <div class="table-head"><span>WHAT TO CHECK</span><span>WHY IT MATTERS</span></div>
              <div><b>Exam year</b><span>Prevents mixing papers from different cycles.</span></div>
              <div><b>Prelims / Mains</b><span>Ensures you are practising the correct stage.</span></div>
              <div><b>Paper number</b><span>Important where the Preliminary scheme has multiple GS papers.</span></div>
              <div><b>Question set/version</b><span>Check the exact version when multiple sets exist.</span></div>
              <div><b>Answer key</b><span>Use an authoritative key where available and distinguish it from unofficial solutions.</span></div>
            </div>

            <p>
              Current third-party indexes show that JPSC previous-paper
              repositories contain papers from multiple cycles and that recent
              Preliminary papers are commonly indexed separately for the
              different General Studies papers. Use these indexes as navigation
              aids, not as a replacement for checking the official record.
              citeturn0search0turn0search3
            </p>

            <h2 id="latest">3. Start With the Latest Available Paper</h2>

            <p>
              Recent papers deserve special attention because they are closest to
              the current examination environment. Search results currently list
              JPSC Preliminary General Studies papers associated with the
              examination held on 19 April 2026, as well as backlog papers from
              May 2026. citeturn0search0turn0search9
            </p>

            <div class="latest">
              <div><b>STEP 1</b><span>Obtain the latest applicable paper.</span></div>
              <div><b>STEP 2</b><span>Attempt it without preparation material.</span></div>
              <div><b>STEP 3</b><span>Record raw score and confidence.</span></div>
              <div><b>STEP 4</b><span>Analyse every question.</span></div>
              <div><b>STEP 5</b><span>Convert findings into your study plan.</span></div>
            </div>

            <div class="warning">
              <strong>Important:</strong>
              Do not copy a “latest paper” from an unverified social-media post.
              Confirm the year, examination and paper identity first.
            </div>

            <h2 id="yearwise">4. How to Use Year-wise JPSC PYQs</h2>

            <p>
              A year-wise collection is useful, but solving papers chronologically
              without analysis wastes much of their value. Use two passes.
            </p>

            <div class="passes">
              <div>
                <span>PASS 1</span>
                <h3>Exam Simulation</h3>
                <p>
                  Attempt the entire paper with a timer. No books, notes or
                  answer-key checking during the attempt.
                </p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <span>PASS 2</span>
                <h3>Analytical Deconstruction</h3>
                <p>
                  Map each question to the syllabus, identify the knowledge
                  tested and record your error type.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <h2 id="paper1">5. Paper 1: How to Analyse General Studies</h2>

            <p>
              Paper 1 should be analysed subject by subject rather than as a
              single score. Depending on the examination cycle, the exact
              composition can vary, so use the paper itself and the official
              syllabus as the basis for classification.
            </p>

            <div class="subjects">
              <div><b>HISTORY</b><span>Ancient, medieval, modern and relevant regional dimensions.</span></div>
              <div><b>GEOGRAPHY</b><span>Physical, India, resources and location-based questions.</span></div>
              <div><b>POLITY</b><span>Constitution, governance, institutions and public administration concepts.</span></div>
              <div><b>ECONOMY</b><span>Indian economy, development, policies and contemporary issues.</span></div>
              <div><b>SCIENCE</b><span>Physics, chemistry, biology, technology and applied science.</span></div>
              <div><b>ENVIRONMENT</b><span>Ecology, conservation, climate and environmental issues.</span></div>
              <div><b>CURRENT AFFAIRS</b><span>National and international events and their static linkages.</span></div>
              <div><b>OTHER</b><span>Classify any question that falls outside your first-pass buckets.</span></div>
            </div>

            <h2 id="paper2">6. Paper 2: Jharkhand-Specific PYQ Analysis</h2>

            <p>
              Paper 2 requires a different analytical lens. Instead of only
              asking “Which subject?”, ask “Which Jharkhand topic?”.
            </p>

            <div class="jharkhand">
              <div><b>HISTORY</b><span>Regional history and important historical developments.</span></div>
              <div><b>MOVEMENT</b><span>Jharkhand Movement, leaders, organisations and statehood.</span></div>
              <div><b>TRIBES</b><span>Society, communities, identity and distinctive features.</span></div>
              <div><b>CULTURE</b><span>Literature, language, dance, music, festivals and heritage.</span></div>
              <div><b>GEOGRAPHY</b><span>Districts, rivers, minerals, forests, climate and resources.</span></div>
              <div><b>LAND LAWS</b><span>CNT, SPT and other syllabus-linked legal provisions.</span></div>
              <div><b>ECONOMY</b><span>Development, industries, policies and displacement.</span></div>
              <div><b>SCHEMES</b><span>State schemes, beneficiaries and implementation.</span></div>
              <div><b>ENVIRONMENT</b><span>Forests, wildlife, climate and conservation.</span></div>
              <div><b>DISASTER</b><span>State-specific hazards, preparedness and response.</span></div>
              <div><b>CURRENT</b><span>Recent state developments linked to static topics.</span></div>
              <div><b>MISC.</b><span>Institutions, sports, education and other syllabus areas.</span></div>
            </div>

            <div class="conversion">
              <div>
                <span>HABITAT IAS • JHARKHAND SPECIAL</span>
                <h2>Don't study Paper 2 as random GK.</h2>
                <p>
                  Map every question to a specific Jharkhand topic and build a
                  revision system around what JPSC has actually tested.
                </p>
              </div>
              <div class="cta-buttons">
                <a class="btn yellow" href="/blog/jpsc-prelims-paper-2-jharkhand-gk-syllabus-strategy-2026/">
                  Jharkhand Paper 2 Guide →
                </a>
                <a class="btn outline" href="/book/">PYQ Book →</a>
              </div>
            </div>

            <h2 id="mapping">7. The Most Important Step: PYQ → Syllabus Mapping</h2>

            <p>
              Suppose a question asks about a particular tribal movement. Do not
              simply mark the answer and move on. Identify the exact syllabus
              node it belongs to and study the surrounding concept.
            </p>

            <div class="mapping">
              <div><b>QUESTION</b><span>Copy a short identifier, not the whole question.</span></div>
              <div><b>SUBJECT</b><span>History / geography / polity / Jharkhand etc.</span></div>
              <div><b>TOPIC</b><span>Specific syllabus heading.</span></div>
              <div><b>CONCEPT</b><span>What knowledge actually solved it?</span></div>
              <div><b>STATUS</b><span>Correct / incorrect / guessed.</span></div>
              <div><b>ACTION</b><span>Study / revise / test again.</span></div>
            </div>

            <h2 id="questiontypes">8. Identify the Type of Question</h2>

            <p>
              Question classification helps you prepare the underlying skill,
              not merely the answer.
            </p>

            <div class="types">
              <div><b>DIRECT FACT</b><span>Tests recall of a specific fact.</span></div>
              <div><b>CONCEPTUAL</b><span>Tests whether you understand a concept.</span></div>
              <div><b>STATEMENT</b><span>Tests discrimination between multiple statements.</span></div>
              <div><b>MATCHING</b><span>Tests associations among several facts.</span></div>
              <div><b>SEQUENCE</b><span>Tests chronology or order.</span></div>
              <div><b>LOCATION</b><span>Tests spatial or geographical association.</span></div>
              <div><b>CURRENT-LINKED</b><span>Uses a recent event to test static knowledge.</span></div>
              <div><b>ELIMINATION</b><span>Can be solved by rejecting incorrect alternatives.</span></div>
            </div>

            <h2 id="solve">9. How to Solve a JPSC PYQ Correctly</h2>

            <div class="solve">
              <div><b>01</b><strong>Timed attempt</strong><span>Replicate examination pressure.</span></div>
              <div><b>02</b><strong>No notes</strong><span>Measure your real preparation.</span></div>
              <div><b>03</b><strong>Mark confidence</strong><span>Separate knowledge from lucky guesses.</span></div>
              <div><b>04</b><strong>Check answer</strong><span>Use the most authoritative answer available.</span></div>
              <div><b>05</b><strong>Study concept</strong><span>Read beyond the one-line answer.</span></div>
              <div><b>06</b><strong>Record error</strong><span>Add the question to your error log.</span></div>
            </div>

            <h2 id="error">10. Build a PYQ Error Log</h2>

            <div class="error-log">
              <div class="head"><span>FIELD</span><span>EXAMPLE</span></div>
              <div><b>Question ID</b><span>2024-P1-Q37</span></div>
              <div><b>Topic</b><span>Modern History</span></div>
              <div><b>Your answer</b><span>B</span></div>
              <div><b>Correct answer</b><span>C</span></div>
              <div><b>Error type</b><span>Concept gap</span></div>
              <div><b>Why wrong?</b><span>Confused two movements</span></div>
              <div><b>Correction</b><span>One-sentence fact + context</span></div>
              <div><b>Retest date</b><span>Next revision cycle</span></div>
            </div>

            <div class="formula">
              <strong>Wrong answer ≠ useless attempt.</strong>
              <span>A wrong answer becomes useful when it changes what you study next.</span>
            </div>

            <h2 id="revision">11. How to Revise PYQs</h2>

            <div class="revision">
              <div><span>R1</span><strong>Same day</strong><p>Understand every wrong and guessed question.</p></div>
              <div><span>R2</span><strong>After 3–7 days</strong><p>Retest only the weak concepts.</p></div>
              <div><span>R3</span><strong>After 2–3 weeks</strong><p>Mix those topics with other subjects.</p></div>
              <div><span>R4</span><strong>Before exam</strong><p>Use the error log for rapid revision.</p></div>
            </div>

            <h2>12. Don't Memorise the PYQ Answer</h2>

            <p>
              This is one of the most common mistakes. If you remember only that
              “option C was correct,” the learning value is very low. Learn the
              concept that made option C correct and understand why the other
              options were wrong.
            </p>

            <div class="deep">
              <div><b>LEVEL 1</b><span>Remember the answer.</span></div>
              <div><b>LEVEL 2</b><span>Understand why it is correct.</span></div>
              <div><b>LEVEL 3</b><span>Understand why alternatives are wrong.</span></div>
              <div><b>LEVEL 4</b><span>Recognise the same concept in a new question.</span></div>
            </div>

            <h2 id="mock">13. PYQ vs Mock Test — Use Both</h2>

            <div class="compare">
              <div>
                <span>PYQ</span>
                <h3>Benchmark</h3>
                <p>Shows the actual historical demand of JPSC.</p>
                <ul>
                  <li>Real examination questions</li>
                  <li>Authentic wording</li>
                  <li>Historical trend</li>
                  <li>Best for analysis</li>
                </ul>
              </div>
              <div>
                <span>MOCK</span>
                <h3>Training</h3>
                <p>Creates additional practice under controlled conditions.</p>
                <ul>
                  <li>Fresh questions</li>
                  <li>Time management</li>
                  <li>Mixed-topic practice</li>
                  <li>Performance tracking</li>
                </ul>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="conversion">
              <div>
                <span>HABITAT IAS • MOCK TESTS</span>
                <h2>Solve the real questions. Then train beyond them.</h2>
                <p>
                  Use PYQs to understand JPSC and mocks to build speed, accuracy
                  and exam stamina.
                </p>
              </div>
              <div class="cta-buttons">
                <a class="btn yellow" href="/mock-tests/free-gs/">Attempt Free Mock →</a>
                <a class="btn outline" href="/mock-tests/">View Mock Tests →</a>
              </div>
            </div>

            <h2 id="book">14. Why a Topic-wise PYQ Book Can Be More Useful</h2>

            <p>
              A normal year-wise paper collection forces you to jump between
              subjects. A topic-wise compilation reorganises the same examination
              history around the syllabus, which makes it easier to identify
              repeated concepts and revise one topic at a time.
            </p>

            <div class="book-benefits">
              <div><b>YEAR-WISE</b><span>Good for full-paper simulation.</span></div>
              <div><b>TOPIC-WISE</b><span>Good for focused learning and revision.</span></div>
              <div><b>SUBJECT-WISE</b><span>Good for identifying subject trends.</span></div>
              <div><b>EXPLANATION</b><span>Good for learning the underlying concept.</span></div>
              <div><b>DATA</b><span>Good for seeing question distribution and priority.</span></div>
            </div>

            <div class="conversion">
              <div>
                <span>HABITAT IAS • JPSC PRELIMS DECODED</span>
                <h2>JPSC PYQs, organised for preparation.</h2>
                <p>
                  Habitat IAS's JPSC Prelims Decoded: Topic-Wise PYQs is designed
                  around topic-wise and subject-wise PYQ study, with explanations
                  and analysis intended to turn past questions into a preparation
                  resource.
                </p>
              </div>
              <div class="cta-buttons">
                <a class="btn yellow" href="/book/">Explore the Book →</a>
                <a class="btn outline" href="/book/#sample">View Sample →</a>
              </div>
            </div>

            <h2>15. PYQ Analysis Dashboard for Your Notebook</h2>

            <div class="dashboard">
              <div><b>TOTAL</b><strong>Questions attempted</strong><span>Track volume.</span></div>
              <div><b>CORRECT</b><strong>Known questions</strong><span>Measure retention.</span></div>
              <div><b>WRONG</b><strong>Knowledge gaps</strong><span>Prioritise repair.</span></div>
              <div><b>GUESSED</b><strong>Low-confidence answers</strong><span>Check real understanding.</span></div>
              <div><b>REPEATED</b><strong>Recurring concepts</strong><span>High revision value.</span></div>
              <div><b>WEAK</b><strong>Topic clusters</strong><span>Plan targeted study.</span></div>
            </div>

            <h2>16. What to Do With a Question You Already Know</h2>

            <p>
              Do not spend equal time on every question. A correct answer that
              you knew confidently may need little additional work. A correct
              answer that you guessed deserves analysis because it represents
              unstable knowledge.
            </p>

            <div class="confidence">
              <div><b>✓ HIGH CONFIDENCE + CORRECT</b><span>Quick review.</span></div>
              <div><b>✓ LOW CONFIDENCE + CORRECT</b><span>Study the concept.</span></div>
              <div><b>✕ HIGH CONFIDENCE + WRONG</b><span>Critical misconception.</span></div>
              <div><b>✕ LOW CONFIDENCE + WRONG</b><span>Knowledge gap.</span></div>
            </div>

            <h2>17. JPSC PYQ Preparation Cycle</h2>

            <div class="cycle">
              <div><b>READ</b><span>Learn the topic.</span></div>
              <i>→</i>
              <div><b>SOLVE</b><span>Attempt PYQs.</span></div>
              <i>→</i>
              <div><b>MAP</b><span>Connect to syllabus.</span></div>
              <i>→</i>
              <div><b>REVISE</b><span>Make short notes.</span></div>
              <i>→</i>
              <div><b>MOCK</b><span>Test under time.</span></div>
              <i>→</i>
              <div><b>REPAIR</b><span>Fix errors.</span></div>
            </div>

            <h2>18. 30-Day PYQ Challenge</h2>

            <div id="plan" class="days">
              <div>
                <span>DAYS 1–7</span>
                <strong>Diagnostic</strong>
                <p>Attempt recent papers and identify your strongest and weakest subjects.</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <span>DAYS 8–15</span>
                <strong>Topic Mapping</strong>
                <p>Classify questions and build topic-wise lists from the syllabus.</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <span>DAYS 16–23</span>
                <strong>Concept Repair</strong>
                <p>Study the weak clusters and retest the questions you missed.</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <span>DAYS 24–30</span>
                <strong>Simulation</strong>
                <p>Attempt full papers and mocks, then analyse the error log.</p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <h2>19. How PYQs Help You Decide What to Study Next</h2>

            <div class="decision">
              <div><b>HIGH FREQUENCY + WEAK</b><span>Highest priority.</span></div>
              <div><b>HIGH FREQUENCY + STRONG</b><span>Maintain through revision.</span></div>
              <div><b>LOW FREQUENCY + WEAK</b><span>Study after core areas.</span></div>
              <div><b>LOW FREQUENCY + STRONG</b><span>Quick revision only.</span></div>
            </div>

            <h2 id="mistakes">20. Common JPSC PYQ Mistakes</h2>

            <div class="mistakes">
              <div><b>01</b><span>Downloading papers but never solving them.</span></div>
              <div><b>02</b><span>Looking at the answer key while attempting.</span></div>
              <div><b>03</b><span>Memorising answers instead of concepts.</span></div>
              <div><b>04</b><span>Ignoring guessed answers in the analysis.</span></div>
              <div><b>05</b><span>Not mapping questions to the syllabus.</span></div>
              <div><b>06</b><span>Using only old papers and ignoring recent cycles.</span></div>
              <div><b>07</b><span>Doing PYQs without maintaining an error log.</span></div>
              <div><b>08</b><span>Treating PYQs as the only source of new practice.</span></div>
              <div><b>09</b><span>Using unverified answer keys as unquestionable truth.</span></div>
              <div><b>10</b><span>Never returning to previously wrong questions.</span></div>
            </div>

            <h2>21. Final JPSC PYQ Checklist</h2>

            <div class="checklist">
              <div><b>01</b><span>Official paper archive bookmarked.</span></div>
              <div><b>02</b><span>Latest relevant Preliminary paper solved.</span></div>
              <div><b>03</b><span>Older papers solved for trend analysis.</span></div>
              <div><b>04</b><span>Paper 1 questions mapped to subjects.</span></div>
              <div><b>05</b><span>Paper 2 questions mapped to Jharkhand topics.</span></div>
              <div><b>06</b><span>Question types classified.</span></div>
              <div><b>07</b><span>Correct, wrong and guessed answers recorded.</span></div>
              <div><b>08</b><span>Error log maintained.</span></div>
              <div><b>09</b><span>Weak topics revised.</span></div>
              <div><b>10</b><span>PYQ-based revision repeated.</span></div>
              <div><b>11</b><span>Fresh mock tests attempted.</span></div>
              <div><b>12</b><span>Mock errors compared with PYQ weaknesses.</span></div>
            </div>

            <h2>22. The Real Value of PYQs</h2>

            <p>
              The objective is not to predict the exact next question. The
              objective is to understand the commission's historical question
              selection, identify recurring knowledge areas, recognise the
              language and structure of questions, and build preparation around
              evidence rather than guesswork.
            </p>

            <div class="final-quote">
              <strong>PYQ tells you where the exam has been.</strong>
              <span>Your preparation decides how ready you are for where it goes next.</span>
            </div>

